eavily cited by MIT scholars and incorporated into the curriculum of the Electrical Engineering and Computer Science (EECS) department, thereby training successive cohorts of students who would go on to push the boundaries of representation learning, reinforcement learning, and unsupervised discovery; indeed, the reinforcement\u2011learning breakthroughs that culminated in AlphaGo\u2019s historic victory over Lee Sedol in 2016 owe a debt to the work of MIT\u2019s own Richard Sutton, whose 1998 textbook \u201cReinforcement Learning: An Introduction\u201d became the field\u2019s bible, and whose later collaborations with DeepMind and OpenAI helped to refine policy\u2011gradient methods, actor\u2011critic architectures, and model\u2011based planning techniques that now underpin autonomous decision\u2011making in robotics, finance, and healthcare, while the university\u2019s emphasis on interdisciplinary research has fostered novel applications of AI in biology through the MIT Center for Brains, Minds and Machines (CBMM), where computational neuroscientists such as James DiCarlo and Tomaso Poggio have leveraged deep\u2011learning models to decode visual cortex activity, leading to the development of neural\u2011inspired algorithms that improve both the interpretability and robustness of AI systems, a synergy that has been further amplified by the Institute\u2019s partnership with the Broad Institute and the Harvard\u2011MIT Health Sciences and Technology (HST) program, which together have produced AI\u2011driven tools for drug discovery, protein\u2011folding prediction, and personalized medicine, exemplified by the 2020 release of AlphaFold\u2019s open\u2011source code and the subsequent MIT\u2011led efforts to integrate its predictions into large\u2011scale biomedical pipelines; beyond pure research, MIT\u2019s role as an incubator for AI\u2011focused entrepreneurship has been institutionalized through initiatives such as the MIT Sandbox Innovation Fund Program, the MI